o the lower bound is the maximum column size among columns not using vertical lines.\n\nThus to minimize total lines, we should use vertical lines for columns with many points, because otherwise we need many distinct lines. Since we have n lines total, we need to ensure that the maximum column size among columns not using vertical lines is ≤ number of non-vertical lines we have (which is n - |V|). Since we have exactly n lines total, and we need to allocate some vertical lines (non-sunny) and some non-vertical lines (including sunny and other non-sunny). The number of non-vertical lines is n - |V|. Among those, we have k sunny lines and (n - |V| - k) other non-sunny lines (horizontal or diagonal). But all non-vertical lines can cover at most one point per column.\n\nThus we need to choose V such that the maximum column size among columns not in V is ≤ n - |V|. Because we need at least that many lines to cover the largest column.\n\nNow, column sizes are decreasing: column 1 has n points, column 2 has n-1, ..., column n has 1 point. So we need to choose V such that the largest column not in V has size ≤ n - |V|.\n\nIf we include vertical lines for columns 1 through t, then |V| = t, and the largest column not in V is column t+1, which has size n - t. So we need n - t ≤ n - t (since n - |V| = n - t). So equality holds. So we can choose any t from 0 to n-1, and the condition holds exactly. So we can have vertical lines for columns 1,...,t, and then we need n - t non-vertical lines to cover the remaining columns t+1,...,n. The largest column among those is column t+1 with size n - t. So we need at least n - t lines, which we have exactly n - t non-vertical lines. So it's possible in principle.\n\nThus we can choose any t, and we can allocate the non-vertical lines to cover the remaining columns. The non-vertical lines can be sunny or non-sunny (horizontal or diagonal). So we can have any number of sunny lines up to n - t (the number of non-vertical lines). So the possible k values are any integer between 0 and n - t inclusive, where t is the number of vertical lines used. But we also need to ensure that the non-vertical lines are distinct and can be arranged to cover all points. Is it always possible to cover the remaining columns with exactly n - t non-vertical lines? Let's examine.\n\nIf we use vertical lines for columns 1,...,t, then we have columns t+1,...,n left. For each column j (t+1 ≤ j ≤ n), we need to cover its points (n+1-j points). Since we have exactly n - t non-vertical lines, we need to assign each line to cover exactly one point in each column (since each line can cover at most one point per column). So each non-vertical line must intersect each of the remaining columns exactly once (i.e., each line must have a point in each column). That would give each line covering exactly (n - t) points (one per column). Since there are n - t columns left, each line would cover one point in each column, giving total coverage (n - t)*(n - t) points. But the total number of points in the remaining columns is sum_{j=t+1}^{n} (n+1-j) = sum_{i=1}^{n-t} i = (n-t)(n-t+1)/2. That's less than (n - t)*(n - t) for n - t > 1. So we have more capacity than needed. So we can have some lines not covering points in some columns? Actually each line can intersect each column at most once, but it could intersect some columns at no point (if the line doesn't intersect the triangle in that column). But we need to cover all points. So we need to allocate lines to cover each point. Since we have more lines than needed? Wait we have exactly n - t lines, and we need to cover (n-t)(n-t+1)/2 points. Each line can cover at most (n - t) points (one per column). So total capacity is (n - t)*(n - t) which is greater than needed for n - t > 1. So capacity is enough. So we can possibly cover all points.\n\nThus the existence of a configuration for any k between 0 and n - t inclusive seems plausible. However, there may be constraints due to the types of lines: non-vertical lines can be horizontal, diagonal, or sunny. Horizontal lines are parallel to x-axis, so they intersect each column at exactly one point (the point with y = constant). Diagonal lines intersect each column at exactly one point (the point where x+y = constant). Sunny lines also intersect each column at at most one point. So any non-vertical line can intersect each column at at most one point. So the above capacity analysis holds.\n\nThus the problem reduces to: For given n, we need to find all k such that there exists a configuration with exactly k sunny lines. Since we can choose any number of sunny lines up to the number of non-vertical lines (n - t). So we need to see if we can achieve any k from 0 up to n - t, for some t. Since t can be any integer from 0 to n (but we need at least one vertical line? Actually we can have t = n, meaning all columns have vertical lines, but then we have n lines all vertical, k=0. That's allowed. For t = n-1, we have vertical lines for columns 1,...,n-1, and one non-vertical line for column n. That non-vertical line can be sunny or non-sunny. So k can be 0 or 1. For t = n-2, we have vertical lines for columns 1,...,n-2, and two non-vertical lines for columns n-1 and n. Those two lines could be both sunny, giving k=2. So maybe k=2 is possible? Let's test.\n\nTake t = n-2. Then we have vertical lines for columns 1,...,n-2 (that's n-2 lines). Then we have two remaining columns: column n-1 (points (n-1,1) and (n-1,2)) and column n (point (n,1)). We have two non-vertical lines left. We need to cover points in these columns. We have two lines, each can intersect each column at most once. So we can try to cover all three points with two lines. Is that possible? Let's see.\n\nWe need to cover (n-1,1), (n-1,2), (n,1). Two lines can cover at most 2 points per column (since each line can intersect column n-1 at most once). So we need to cover both points in column n-1 using the two lines (one point per line). And we need to cover (n,1) using one of the lines (maybe the same line that covers (n-1,1) or (n-1,2)). So we need to find two lines L1 and L2 such that:\n\n- L1 passes through (n-1,1) and maybe (n,1) or (n-1,2) or something else.\n- L2 passes through the remaining point(s).\n\nWe also need exactly k sunny lines. If we want k=2, both L1 and L2 must be sunny (i.e., not horizontal, vertical, or slope -1). Let's see if we can find two sunny lines covering these three points.\n\nOption 1: L1 = line through (n-1,1) and (n,1). That's horizontal line y=1, which is non-sunny (parallel to x-axis). So that would be a non-sunny line. Then L2 must cover (n-1,2). L2 could be any line not horizontal, vertical, or slope -1. For example, line through (n-1,2) and (1,1) (slope = (2-1)/(n-1-1) = 1/(n-2) which is not 0,∞,-1). That's sunny. So we would have one non-sunny (horizontal) and one sunny. So k=1, not 2.\n\nOption 2: L1 = line through (n-1,2) and (n,1). Slope = (1-2)/(n-(n-1)) = -1/1 = -1. That's slope -1, which is non-sunny (parallel to x+y=0). So L1 is non-sunny. L2 must cover (n-1,1). Could be sunny line through (n-1,1) and (1,1) (horizontal) which is non-sunny. Or line through (n-1,1) and (1,2) slope = (2-1)/(1-(n-1)) = 1/(-n+2) = -1/(n-2) which is not 0,∞,-1 (unless n=2, but n≥3). So that line is sunny. So we have one sunny (L2) and one non-sunny (L1). So k=1.\n\nOption 3: L1 = line through (n-1,1) and (n-1,2) is vertical line x=n-1, which is non-sunny. L2 must cover (n,1). Could be sunny line through (n,1) and (1,1) (horizontal) non-sunny, or (n,1) and (1,2) slope = (2-1)/(1-n) = 1/(1-n) = -1/(n-1) which is not 0,∞,-1 (for n≥3). So L2 could be sunny. So again k=1.\n\nThus with t=n-2, we cannot have both lines sunny because at least one of the lines covering the points must be horizontal or slope -1 or vertical. Let's examine all possibilities: We need to cover three points with two lines. If both lines are sunny, they cannot be horizontal, vertical, or slope -1. So we need to find two sunny lines that together cover (n-1,1), (n-1,2), (n,1). Let's try to find two sunny lines L1 and L2 such that each point is on at least one of them. Since there are three points, at least one line must contain two of them. So we need a sunny line that contains two of the three points. Let's check each pair:\n\n- Pair (n-1,1) and (n-1,2): vertical line x=n-1, slope ∞, non-sunny. So cannot be sunny.\n\n- Pair (n-1,1) and (n,1): horizontal line y=1, slope 0, non-sunny.\n\n- Pair (n-1,2) and (n,1): slope = (1-2)/(n-(n-1)) = -1, slope -1, non-sunny.\n\nThus no pair of these three points lies on a sunny line. Therefore any line covering two of them must be non-sunny. So at least one of the two lines must be non-sunny. Therefore k cannot be 2. So k=2 is impossible. This matches the solution's claim.\n\nThus the impossibility of k=2 is correct.\n\nNow k=3: The solution says construction exists using three sunny lines. Let's see if we can construct with exactly 3 sunny lines. For n≥3, we need to find a configuration with exactly 3 sunny lines and n-3 non-sunny lines. The solution does not provide explicit construction, but we need to verify if such a construction exists. We need to see if it's possible.\n\nWe can try to generalize the construction for k=3. For n≥3, we can use vertical lines for columns 1,...,n-3 (that's n-3 vertical lines). Then we have three remaining columns: n-2, n-1, n. Column n-2 has 3 points, column n-1 has 2 points, column n has 1 point. We have three non-vertical lines left (since total lines = n). We need to cover these 6 points with three lines, each line can intersect each column at most once. So we need to cover each column's points. Since column n-2 has 3 points, we need at least 3 lines to cover them (since no line can cover more than one point in that column). So we need exactly three lines, each covering one point in column n-2. Then those lines can also cover points in columns n-1 and n. So we need to assign each of the three points in column n-2 to