al number of points is sum_{a=1}^{n} (n+1-a) = n(n+1)/2. Indeed, the number of points is n(n+1)/2.\n\nWe need to cover all these points with n distinct lines. Each line can be any line, but we have constraints: exactly k of them are sunny (i.e., not parallel to x-axis, y-axis, or line x+y=0). The other n-k lines can be non-sunny, i.e., they can be parallel to x-axis (horizontal), y-axis (vertical), or line x+y=0 (i.e., slope -1). So the non-sunny lines are allowed to be horizontal, vertical, or slope -1 lines.\n\nWe need to find all possible k for which such a covering exists.\n\nWe need to consider the constraints: we have n lines covering n(n+1)/2 points. Each line can cover many points. The lines must be distinct. The non-sunny lines are limited to three families: horizontal (y = constant), vertical (x = constant), or slope -1 (x + y = constant). The sunny lines can be any other slope (i.e., slopes not 0, infinite, or -1). So they can be any line not parallel to those three families.\n\nWe need to find possible k.\n\nWe need to check if k=0 is possible: all lines are non-sunny, i.e., each line is either horizontal, vertical, or slope -1. The solution says use n vertical lines. Let's see if n vertical lines can cover all points. The points are (a,b) with a+b ≤ n+1. For each a from 1 to n, there are points (a,b) for b = 1,..., n+1-a. So each vertical line x = a (for a = 1,...,n) will contain all points with that x-coordinate, i.e., (a,b) for b from 1 to n+1-a. So indeed, the vertical line x = a covers all points with that a. So using vertical lines x = 1, x = 2, ..., x = n, we cover all points. So k=0 is possible.\n\nNow k=1: exactly one sunny line, the rest n-1 non-sunny lines. The solution says use 1 sunny + (n-1) verticals. But we need to check if that works: we have n-1 vertical lines covering all points except maybe some points that are not covered by those vertical lines. But we have n vertical lines originally covering all points. If we replace one vertical 