=n-2, n-1, n and y≥2 (except those covered by y=1). Let's list all points not covered by vertical lines x=1,...,x=n-3 and horizontal line y=1.\n\nVertical lines x=1,...,x=n-3 cover all points with a ≤ n-3. So points with a = n-2, n-1, n remain.\n\nHorizontal line y=1 covers all points with b=1, regardless of a. So it covers (n-2,1), (n-1,1), (n,1). So those are covered.\n\nNow remaining points are:\n\n- For a=n-2: b can be 2,3 (since b ≤ n+1-(n-2) = 3). So points (n-2,2), (n-2,3).\n\n- For a=n-1: b can be 2 (since b ≤ n+1-(n-1) = 2). So point (n-1,2).\n\n- For a=n: b can be 1 only, but that's covered by y=1.\n\nThus remaining points: (n-2,2), (n-2,3), (n-1,2). That's three points left. We have two sunny lines left. Can we cover these three points with two sunny lines? Let's see.\n\nPoints: (n-2,2), (n-2,3), (n-1,2). As before, any line through any two of these points is either vertical (x=n-2) for (n-2,2)-(n-2,3), horizontal (y=2) for (n-2,2)-(n-1,2), or slope -1 for (n-2,3)-(n-1,2). So none are sunny. So we cannot cover them with sunny lines. So this fails.\n\nThus we need to adjust the set of non-sunny lines to cover some of these points as well, leaving a set that can be covered by sunny lines.\n\nMaybe we can use a slope -1 line as a non-sunny line to cover some of these points. For example, line x+y = n+1 (slope -1) covers points (n-2,3), (n-1,2), (n,1). But (n,1) is already covered by y=1. So we could use x+y = n+1 as a non-sunny line, covering (n-2,3) and (n-1,2). Then we would have left point (n-2,2). That's one point left, which can be covered by a sunny line. But we need exactly two sunny lines, not one. However, we could use the second sunny line to cover some other points that we haven't covered yet, maybe some points with a ≤ n-3 that we could have covered with vertical lines but we could replace some vertical lines with sunny lines. But we need exactly n lines total, with exactly two sunny lines. So we could have n-3 vertical lines, one horizontal line