over them. Let's see if we can cover these four points with two sunny lines.\n\nPoints left: (n-2,1), (n-2,3), (n-1,2), (n,1).\n\nWe need to find two sunny lines that cover these four points. Let's try to pair them.\n\n- Pair (n-2,1) with (n-1,2): slope = (2-1)/(n-1-(n-2)) = 1/1 = 1, sunny. So line L1: y = x + (3-n) (as before). This line covers (n-2,1) and (n-1,2). Does it also cover any other points? It might also pass through (n,3) but that's outside region. So L1 covers those two points.\n\n- Remaining points: (n-2,3) and (n,1). The line through them has slope (1-3)/(n-(n-2)) = -2/2 = -1, not sunny. So we cannot cover them with a single sunny line. Could we instead pair (n-2,3) with (n-2,1) (vertical) not allowed. Pair (n-2,3) with (n-1,2) slope -1, not allowed. Pair (n-2,3) with (n,1) slope -1, not allowed. So we cannot cover (n-2,3) and (n,1) with a sunny line. So this fails.\n\nTry x+y = n-1, covering (n-2,1). Then remaining points: (n-2,2), (n-2,3), (n-1,1), (n-1,2), (n,1). That's five points left, with two sunny lines, impossible.\n\nThus using v=n-3 vertical lines and one slope -1 line fails.\n\nMaybe we can use a horizontal line instead of a slope -1 line. Let's try v=n-3 vertical lines, h=1 horizontal line, s=2 sunny lines. So total lines = n-3+1+2 = n. Good.\n\nWe need to choose a horizontal line y = d to cover many of the points with a = n-2, n-1, n.\n\nPoints as before: (n-2,1), (n-2,2), (n-2,3), (n-1,1), (n-1,2), (n,1).\n\nPick horizontal line y=1: covers (n-2,1), (n-1,1), (n,1). Remaining points: (n-2,2), (n-2,3), (n-1,2). That's three points left, with two sunny lines. As before, any line through any two of these points is vertical/horizontal/slope -1, not sunny. So fails.\n\nPick horizontal line y=2: covers (n-2,2), (n-1,2). Remaining points: (n-2,1), (n-2,3), (n-1,1), (n,1). That's four points left, with two sunny lines. Let's see if we can cover them.\n\nPoints: (n-2,1), (n-2,3), (n-1,1), (n,1).\n\nWe can try to pair (n-2,1) with (n-1,1): horizo